module Native

{-
   The instructions that are one machine's rather than an operation the language has - x86's, and
   nothing that is not one.

   Everything in `Core` is portable: a name there means the same thing on every target, and where a
   target has no instruction for it a backend writes what it does have. **This file is the
   exception**, and there is one of it per processor family. `Intrinsic/X86.yana` is x86's; an ARM
   one joins it beside rather than inside it, under its own namespace and its own attributes, and
   nothing outside this directory declares an instruction at all.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Why `Native`, and why a namespace inside it

   A file of module `Native`, two directories deep, which is what `module Native` above says
   (Analysis-Modules.md §2.1). `Native` is the unsafe half of the prelude and the one module that is
   **not** implicitly imported, so naming an instruction costs an `import Native` - which is exactly
   the statement a program should have to make to leave portable ground. `Core` is implicitly
   imported and is therefore the wrong place: a name reachable by writing nothing must mean the same
   thing on every target.

   `X86.` is a namespace and not a module - doc/spec/modules.md. A module would give these a `pub`
   boundary they have no interface behind, a second import every user of them would write, and a
   coherence unit; what a prefix is wanted for here is naming and nothing else. It has to be there
   because a reader of `Digest/ShaBlock.sha.avx2.yana` must be able to tell an instruction from a
   function: `sha1Rounds(abcd, e, 0)` reads as a library call and is a single opcode with a trailing
   byte, and `X86.sha1Rounds(abcd, e, 0)` says which manual to open.

   The names are the machine's, spelled the way this language spells names. Anyone reading a body
   that uses them is reading against Intel's manual, and a name invented here would only stand
   between the two.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Why availability is an attribute per declaration and not a selector on the file name

   Every declaration below carries the target it needs, and there are three different answers in one
   file: `@platform(x64)` for what an x86-64 build always has, `@platform(avx2)` for what the AVX tier
   supplies, and `@platform(sha)` for what an extension beside the level answers for. **A declaration
   an attribute excluded is never resolved**, which is the whole of what the gating owes: a build
   without the instruction must not resolve a body naming it, and it cannot, because there is no
   declaration for such a body to name.

   A file-name selector (`X86.sha.yana`) says one thing about *every* declaration in a file at once,
   and three answers is exactly why that is the wrong shape here - `crc32` and `vzeroupper` have
   nothing to do with the SHA extension, and a file selected on `sha` deletes them from every build
   that does not claim it. One attribute per declaration says which instruction needs what, and
   adding a second extension adds a declaration rather than a file.

   What is still a *file* is code written **in terms of** these - `Digest/ShaBlock.sha.avx2.yana`,
   which holds replacements of two portable functions (Design-Replacements.md). That is a body rather
   than a declaration, and the distinction is load-bearing: a body kept behind a compile-time
   `if hasShaExtension()` leaves the untaken arm **resolved**. It is IR, it reaches lowering in any
   build with the IR optimizer off (`-no-opt`, which every fixture in this tree is also compiled
   with), and the backend then refuses instructions the target cannot encode. See `targetSelector`
   in compiler/compiler/settings.cpp, which answers both spellings so that an attribute and a file
   name can never disagree about what a target is called.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Why there is nothing portable behind them

   Every other machine-specific operation this compiler knows about is an *arrangement of bits* a
   backend can write out longhand: `pext` is a loop, `lzcnt` is a scan and a subtraction, `movbe` is
   a load and a reversal. Nothing here is. `sha256rnds2` is two rounds of a hash and `crc32` is a
   named polynomial, so a backend that expanded either would hold a second implementation of a
   published algorithm inside the compiler, tested by nothing and free to drift from the library's.
   There is therefore no expansion behind any of these at any tier, and what stands in for them is a
   different implementation of the same function: the portable loops in `Digest/Sha1.yana` and
   `Digest/Sha2.yana`, which the hardware bodies replace, and the byte table in `Digest/Crc.yana` for
   the checksum.
-}

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `@platform(x64)` - what an x86-64 target has by having been x86-64.
   ==========================================================================================

   The CRC-32C step. SSE4.2, and therefore x86-64-v2, and therefore this backend's floor:
   `kFeatureBaseline` *is* v2 (codegen/x64/target.h), so there is no feature name beside the
   architecture here and no tier below this one to fall back to.

   `X86.crc32(remainder, chunk)` folds `chunk` into the running remainder under the **Castagnoli**
   polynomial `0x11EDC6F41`, reflected. That is the polynomial in the silicon and there is no
   parameter for another: the IEEE polynomial that zip, gzip, PNG and Ethernet mean is a different
   function this instruction cannot compute, and a caller who wants it wants the table in
   `Digest/Crc.yana`. The two live side by side there, and the note above them is where the
   difference is written down rather than here.

   **One width, and it is sixty-four.** The machine has `crc32 r32/r64, r/m8/16/32/64`; what a
   checksum over a run of bytes wants is the widest of those, and the narrower ones are not
   declared because nothing above the backend can select one - the lower IR has no scalar below
   `Int32`, so a byte-wide chunk cannot be told apart from a 32-bit one. A tail of fewer than eight
   bytes reaches the table instead, which costs a handful of lookups once per message and nothing
   per block.

   **The remainder is at the chunk's width rather than at thirty-two**, which is what the machine
   does: the 64-bit form reads its remainder from the low half of its destination and writes a
   zero-extended answer, so the `widen` and the `truncate` a caller writes around it are conversions
   the instruction already performs and both fold away. `crcWide` in `Digest/Crc.native.yana` is
   that caller and is where it shows.
-}
@builtin(x86Crc32) @platform(x64) pub fn X86.crc32(remainder: U64, chunk: U64) -> U64
@builtin(x86Nop) @platform(x64) pub fn X86.nop() -> {}
@builtin(x86Lretq) @platform(x64) pub fn X86.lretq(codeSegment: U16) -> {}

{-
   The x86 system and machine-state instructions. These are exposed as raw operations because their
   meaning is the processor's rather than the language's. The fixed-register instructions that
   return more than one scalar write through caller-provided pointers so one source call still
   emits exactly one instruction. `lretq` is the deliberate exception: its backend constructs a
   same-privilege far-return frame for the supplied code selector and resumes after that frame, so
   a source call changes CS without requiring a naked function or exposing the temporary stack use.
-}
@builtin(x86Cpuid) @platform(x64) pub fn X86.cpuid(leaf: U32, subleaf: U32, eax: %U32, ebx: %U32, ecx: %U32, edx: %U32) -> {}
@builtin(x86Rdtscp) @platform(x64) pub fn X86.rdtscp(low: %U32, high: %U32) -> {}
@builtin(x86Rdtsc) @platform(x64) pub fn X86.rdtsc() -> U64
@builtin(x86MFence) @platform(x64) pub fn X86.mfence() -> {}
@builtin(x86LFence) @platform(x64) pub fn X86.lfence() -> {}
