{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `@platform(avx2)` - the AVX tier.
   ==========================================================================================

   `vzeroupper` - the vector state reset.

   Clears the upper half of every vector register. What it is for is the state a processor is in when
   it executes a *legacy-encoded* SSE instruction while some upper half is non-zero, which costs a
   large and silent penalty on every part this matters on - so a function whose body is encoded
   without a VEX prefix wants this to have happened before its first instruction runs.

   **`v3` and not `x64`**, because the instruction is AVX's: below that tier there is no encoding
   that could have left an upper half non-zero, nothing to clear, and no instruction to write. A
   level selector is answered as "at least", so this exists on a v3 or a v4 build and nowhere else.
   `avx` is not a spelling for it here for the reason it is not one on the command line either - AVX
   alone was Sandy Bridge, which this compiler describes as v2.

   **A call rather than something the backend emits at the entry of such a function**, and the reason
   is that the two are about different sets of functions. `@x86_legacy_sse` is about *encoding* and
   belongs on every function of a region; clearing the state may only happen where no vector value is
   arriving, which is the region's entry points and not the helpers below them. A rule deriving one
   from the other would read a signature and be right until somebody added a vector parameter, at
   which point it would stop firing and say nothing.

   **It is destructive, and where it is safe is the caller's convention.** Every register it clears
   has to be one nobody is holding a value in: `@convention(clobber)` is what says so for the caller,
   since nothing survives one of its calls, and having no vector parameter is what says so for this
   function. Written anywhere else it is corruption of a value nothing named.

   On LLVM this is dropped - that backend chooses its own encodings and places its own resets. See
   Analysis-Warts.md §7 and `legacyVectorEncodings` in compiler/codegen/x64/target.h.
-}
@builtin(x86VZeroUpper) @platform(avx2) pub fn X86.vzeroupper() -> {}

{-
   `vzeroall` - the stronger vector state reset. It clears every vector register, including the low
   halves that `vzeroupper` leaves intact. Unlike `vzeroupper`, this is a security/state-transition
   primitive rather than an ABI optimization: every backend must preserve the operation, and only the
   caller knows that no vector value is live across the reset. The x64 backend checks that promise.
-}
@builtin(x86VZeroAll) @platform(avx2) pub fn X86.vzeroall() -> {}

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The SHA extension - `@platform(sha)`, seven instructions.
   ==========================================================================================

   Not inside any level: a v3 or v4 machine may or may not have these, so `sha` is a selector of its
   own and `-enable-inst sha` or a host that reports it is what turns them on. There is no negation
   of it: a build without the extension runs the portable body these replace; see `targetSelector`.

   **The digests also need v3**, which is a fact about the library rather than about the silicon:
   `Digest/ShaBlock.sha.avx2.yana` is a legacy-encoded region and is therefore written in terms of
   `X86.vzeroupper()` above, and its name says so. Goldmont shipped these instructions with no AVX at
   all; such a part has the declarations here and takes the portable compression, which is the whole
   of what it gives up.

   **Why the operands say `Vec(U32, 4)`.** Not `Vec(U32)`, whose lane count is the target's, and not
   a const parameter: these instructions read 128 bits as the four words a SHA state or message
   schedule is made of, there is no other width, and a signature that suggested otherwise would be
   describing an instruction that does not exist.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   SHA-1, four instructions.

   `sha1Message1(w0_3, w4_7)` and `sha1Message2(w16_19, w13_16)` are the two halves of the message
   schedule: the first performs the exclusive-ors that do not need a rotation, the second finishes
   them and rotates. Between them a caller does the one `xor` the pair does not - the schedule needs
   four inputs and each instruction reads two.

   `sha1NextE(e, state)` adds the `E` working variable into the top word of the next round group,
   which is the one part of SHA-1's state that does not live in the four-word register with the rest.

   `sha1Rounds(abcd, e, group)` performs four rounds. `group` selects which of the four round
   functions - 0 for the choice, 1 and 3 for the parity, 2 for the majority - and **must be a
   literal**: it is the instruction's trailing byte, so a computed one is not an instruction. It is
   checked where it is written, which is the only place that can name the argument.
-}
@builtin(x86Sha1Message1) @platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha1Message1(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@builtin(x86Sha1Message2) @platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha1Message2(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@builtin(x86Sha1NextE) @platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha1NextE(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@builtin(x86Sha1Rounds) @platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha1Rounds(abcd: Vec(U32, 4), e: Vec(U32, 4), group: Int) -> Vec(U32, 4)

{-
   SHA-256, three instructions.

   `sha256Message1(w0_3, w4_7)` and `sha256Message2(w16_19, w12_15)` are the message schedule's two
   halves, on SHA-1's pattern: the first applies the small sigma to four words, the second finishes
   the sum and applies the other sigma. The `+ w[i-7]` in the middle is the caller's, and it is one
   `add` and one `alignLanes`.

   `sha256Rounds(state, feed, keys)` performs **two full rounds**. `keys` is the two message words
   with their round constants already added, and on the machine it is an implicit operand read out of
   `xmm0` - which is a register-allocation constraint the backend carries and a caller never sees.
   `state` and `feed` are the eight working variables in two registers, in the order the instruction
   wants them rather than in the order the specification names them: `{C, D, G, H}` and
   `{A, B, E, F}`, which is why the compression function shuffles once on the way in and once on the
   way out.
-}
@builtin(x86Sha256Message1) @platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha256Message1(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@builtin(x86Sha256Message2) @platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha256Message2(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@builtin(x86Sha256Rounds) @platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha256Rounds(state: Vec(U32, 4), feed: Vec(U32, 4), keys: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   AES-NI - `@platform(aes)`, six instructions, and VAES's four two-block forms beside them.
   ==========================================================================================

   A flag beside the level rather than inside one (Analysis-Avx512.md §0.3): nameable at `sse4`,
   where Nehalem and the low-end Westmere-to-Ivy-Bridge parts do not have it, and implied from
   `avx2` up. A build without it has no declarations here, and what a cipher runs there is the
   library's portable body rather than any expansion.

   **Unlike the SHA extension, every one of these has a VEX spelling** (`vaesenc`), so a function
   using them is an ordinary function: no `@x86_legacy_sse`, no `X86.vzeroupper()`, and at `sse4`
   the whole program is legacy-encoded anyway.

   **Why the operands say `Vec(U8, 16)`.** A block of AES is sixteen bytes and the state is those
   bytes in column order, which is exactly the order a block is loaded in - so a block read out of a
   message is the operand without a shuffle. There is no other width at this flag.

   `aesEncrypt(state, key)` is one full round of the cipher - ShiftRows, SubBytes, MixColumns and
   the exclusive-or with `key` - and `aesEncryptLast(state, key)` the last round, which has no
   MixColumns. An AES-128 encryption is the whitening `xor` with round key 0, nine `aesEncrypt`s and
   one `aesEncryptLast`.

   `aesDecrypt(state, key)` and `aesDecryptLast(state, key)` are the same two rounds of the
   *equivalent* inverse cipher (FIPS-197 §5.3.5), whose round keys are the encryption schedule
   reversed with `aesInverseMix` applied to every key but the first and the last.

   `aesInverseMix(key)` is InvMixColumns of one round key - that conversion, and nothing else.

   `aesKeygenAssist(key, roundConstant)` is the key schedule's step: SubWord of words 1 and 3 of
   `key` in words 0 and 2 of the answer, and RotWord of each with `roundConstant` exclusive-ored into
   the low byte in words 1 and 3. `roundConstant` **must be a literal** from 0 to 255 - it is the
   instruction's trailing byte - and what an AES key schedule writes is the ten of FIPS-197's `Rcon`.
-}
@builtin(x86AesEncrypt) @platform(aes) pub fn X86.aesEncrypt(state: Vec(U8, 16), key: Vec(U8, 16)) -> Vec(U8, 16)
@builtin(x86AesEncryptLast) @platform(aes) pub fn X86.aesEncryptLast(state: Vec(U8, 16), key: Vec(U8, 16)) -> Vec(U8, 16)
@builtin(x86AesDecrypt) @platform(aes) pub fn X86.aesDecrypt(state: Vec(U8, 16), key: Vec(U8, 16)) -> Vec(U8, 16)
@builtin(x86AesDecryptLast) @platform(aes) pub fn X86.aesDecryptLast(state: Vec(U8, 16), key: Vec(U8, 16)) -> Vec(U8, 16)
@builtin(x86AesInverseMix) @platform(aes) pub fn X86.aesInverseMix(key: Vec(U8, 16)) -> Vec(U8, 16)
@builtin(x86AesKeygenAssist) @platform(aes) pub fn X86.aesKeygenAssist(key: Vec(U8, 16), roundConstant: Int) -> Vec(U8, 16)

{-
   VAES - `@platform(vaes)`: the four rounds over **two blocks at once**, each 128-bit half of the
   operands an independent block with its own round key. Named `vaes` beside `avx2` (Zen 3, Alder
   Lake and later have it without AVX-512) and implied at `avx512`.

   What these are for is the block-parallel modes - CTR, the counter half of GCM, XTS - where
   consecutive blocks do not depend on each other and two of them go through each instruction. A
   `2` rather than a generic width because a declaration is what an attribute gates: the 128-bit
   rounds exist wherever `aes` does, and these only where `vaes` does. The 512-bit forms, four
   blocks at once, wait for the 64-byte natural width (Analysis-Avx512.md §1.2).

   The key schedule has no wide form on the machine; a caller splats each round key into both halves.
-}
@builtin(x86AesEncrypt2) @platform(vaes) pub fn X86.aesEncrypt2(state: Vec(U8, 32), key: Vec(U8, 32)) -> Vec(U8, 32)
@builtin(x86AesEncryptLast2) @platform(vaes) pub fn X86.aesEncryptLast2(state: Vec(U8, 32), key: Vec(U8, 32)) -> Vec(U8, 32)
@builtin(x86AesDecrypt2) @platform(vaes) pub fn X86.aesDecrypt2(state: Vec(U8, 32), key: Vec(U8, 32)) -> Vec(U8, 32)
@builtin(x86AesDecryptLast2) @platform(vaes) pub fn X86.aesDecryptLast2(state: Vec(U8, 32), key: Vec(U8, 32)) -> Vec(U8, 32)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   PCLMULQDQ - `@platform(pclmul)`, and VPCLMULQDQ's two-block form beside it.
   ==========================================================================================

   The carryless multiply: one 64 x 64 product of polynomials over GF(2), 127 bits in a 128-bit
   answer. What it completes is CRC folding - CRC32 (IEEE), CRC64 and CRC32C past the one-stream
   `crc32` chain - and GHASH/POLYVAL for AES-GCM (Analysis-Avx512.md §4.2). The same flag rules as
   `aes`: nameable at `sse4`, implied from `avx2` up.

   `carrylessMultiply(a, b, selector)` multiplies one quadword of `a` by one of `b`. `selector` is the
   machine's own trailing byte and **must be a literal**, one of four: bit 0 picks `a`'s quadword and
   bit 4 picks `b`'s, so `0x00` is low by low, `0x11` high by high, `0x01` the high quadword of `a` by
   the low one of `b`, and `0x10` the other way round. Anything else is refused rather than masked,
   since the instruction ignores the other six bits and a program that set one meant something else.

   `carrylessMultiply2` is VPCLMULQDQ (`@platform(vpclmul)`, named beside `avx2` or implied at
   `avx512`): the same selector applied to each 128-bit half independently, which is two products per
   instruction - the folding step of a two-stream CRC.
-}
@builtin(x86CarrylessMultiply) @platform(pclmul) pub fn X86.carrylessMultiply(a: Vec(U64, 2), b: Vec(U64, 2), selector: Int) -> Vec(U64, 2)
@builtin(x86CarrylessMultiply2) @platform(vpclmul) pub fn X86.carrylessMultiply2(a: Vec(U64, 4), b: Vec(U64, 4), selector: Int) -> Vec(U64, 4)
